      "id": 1,
      "text": "Omonia Square first appeared on maps in 1862, its name emerging from political movements that sought unity.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.athenssocialatlas.gr/en/article/omonia-concorde/"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "In Kalergis’ 1865 plan, the square was labeled “Omonia Square – Place de la Concorde,” linking it to Parisian Concorde.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.athenssocialatlas.gr/en/article/omonia-concorde/"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The square’s evolution from Anaktoron to Otto Platz to Omonia mirrors Greece’s transition from Ottoman rule to a modern nation-state.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.athenssocialatlas.gr/en/article/omonia-concorde/"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Kalergis’ map records Omonia Square as “Place de la Concorde,” showing 19th‑century Athens’ aspiration to align with European capitals.",
